<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dzungars</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dzungar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dzungars were a confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> Mongol tribes who formed the last great nomadic empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-asia">Inner Asia</a> in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. They fought a series of wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, until the Qing destroyed their khanate in the 1750s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dzungars.jpg" alt="Dzungars" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1769, China · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ili_region_Taiji_(Mongol_Prince)_and_his_wife,_Huang_Qing_Zhigong_Tu,_1769.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chagataid line itself survived in these successors until their absorption by the <b>Dzungars</b> in the late seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries — but as an independent governing entity, the khanate that had once commanded the central Silk Road from the Amu Darya to the Altai had dissolved…</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Dzungars</b> cast cannon with the help of Johan Gustaf Renat, a Swedish officer captured by Russia at Poltava in 1709 who later fell into Dzungar hands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1643 Jahangir Khan, with a small force of musketeers and help from Yalangtush Bahadur of Samarkand, is said to have checked the <b>Dzungar</b> ruler Erdeni Batur at the Battle of Orbulaq in the mountains of Zhetysu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Dzungar</b> campaigns of 1755–1758 brought the near-complete destruction of the Dzungar Mongols through military violence, deliberate massacre and smallpox, an episode that many historians characterise as the Dzungar genocide, in which perhaps several hundred thousand people perished.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1717 many Bonpos were executed during the <b>Dzungar</b> invasion.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bon">Bon · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Massacres ordered under the Qianlong Emperor, smallpox and flight destroyed most of the <b>Dzungar</b> people.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
